{"name":"Newsprint","hex":"#756f6d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"newsprint","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An inexpensive paper used for printing newspapers."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/newsprint"}],"entries":[{"title":"Newsprint","blocks":[{"text":"Newsprint is a low-cost, non-archival type of paper, consisting mainly of wood pulp and most commonly used to print newspapers and other publications and advertising material. Invented in 1844 by Charles Fenerty of Nova Scotia, Canada, it usually has an off-white cast and distinctive feel. It is designed for use in printing presses that employ a long web of paper, rather than individual sheets of paper."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Rolls_of_finished_newsprint_at_Macmillan-Bloedel_Ltd_in_the_Fraser_River_Valley%2C_British_Columbia%2C_Canada.JPG/330px-Rolls_of_finished_newsprint_at_Macmillan-Bloedel_Ltd_in_the_Fraser_River_Valley%2C_British_Columbia%2C_Canada.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newsprint","wikidata":"Q187046"}],"images":[],"sounds":{"newsprint":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f9/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-newsprint.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/f/f9/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-newsprint.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-newsprint.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/f/f9/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-newsprint.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-newsprint.wav.ogg"}]}}}